WEBVTT
TITLE Function Pointers — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Three functions, three addresses in the code segment. The instructions live here, not in the data segment.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A function pointer is an ordinary variable. Its value is one of those addresses, and it lives in the data segment.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Three ways to write the assignment, and all three store the same address: = add, = &add, = *add.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
sizeof p is eight bytes. sizeof add is not allowed at all: a function type has no size to report.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
GATE CSE 2005 asked what int (*f)(int *) declares, and offered four English sentences.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Step one: find the identifier. It is f, and everything else describes f.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Step two: the star inside the parentheses makes f a pointer. Step three: (int *) says it points at a function taking int *.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Step four: what is left is the return type, int. So f is a pointer to function taking (int *) returning int — option C.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
int *f(int) is a function taking an int and returning a pointer to int.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
int (*f)(int) is a pointer to a function taking an int and returning int. One pair of parentheses, a different type.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
int (*f[3])(int) is an array of three pointers to functions. It is a table, not a pointer to a table.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
int (**f)(int) is a pointer to a pointer to function: you can repoint which pointer is used.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The grammar is short: a declarator is an optional pointer followed by a direct-declarator.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
In int *f(int), the parameter list attaches first: f is a function, then the pointer applies to what it returns.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
In int (*f)(int), the star attaches first: f is a pointer, and the function is what it points at.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
So the reading order is the constructor order, and the base type is always applied last.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
This is the declaration of signal in signal.h. It is the hardest one the standard library asks you to read.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Step one: signal followed by a parameter list, so signal is a function taking an int and a handler.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Step two: the star before signal makes the result a pointer, and the trailing (int) says what it points at.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The full reading, then one typedef that collapses it: typedef void (*handler)(int); handler signal(int, handler);

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A pointer cell holding 0x401136, and the function it points at. Calling through it is one extra load.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
f(3) and (*f)(3) are the same call. The star is undone by the designator rule.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
But *p(3) is *(p(3)): it calls p first and dereferences the int it returns. That is not a call at all.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
f, &f and *f all name 0x401136. But &p is the address of the cell, not the code.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Six integers, and a comparator the sort will call with pointers to two of them.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The contract: negative if the first is less, zero if equal, positive if greater. Nothing else is promised.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Under the correct comparator this array sorts in eleven comparisons — inside the five to fifteen bounds.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Equal elements may come out in either order: qsort is not stable. The two 3s are not interchangeable.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The most common comparator is return a - b. With a two billion and b minus two billion, that is four billion.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Four billion does not fit in an int. It wraps to minus two hundred ninety-four million, so the sign is wrong.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The second broken comparator returns a > b, which is 1 or 0 and never negative.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The repair is one line: return (a > b) - (a < b). Three outcomes, no arithmetic to overflow.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Four function pointers in one array: add, sub, mul, div. The opcode is the index.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The program adds five, multiplies by three, subtracts two. Table lookup and switch both give thirteen.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The model ran all one thousand seven hundred twenty-eight programs of three steps both ways, and they agreed every time.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Cost: an if-chain over four operators averages two and a half tests per instruction, the table one. Over a thousand instructions that is 2500 against 1000.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Calling through a converted pointer of the wrong type is undefined behaviour.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Arithmetic on a function pointer is not allowed: addition needs a pointer to a complete object type.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
sizeof of a function is a constraint violation; sizeof of the pointer is the pointer size.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
A parameter written with a function type is adjusted to a pointer. Inside the callee, &cb is a pointer to a pointer.

